    "updated_at": "2026-06-30T20:34:47Z",
    "body": "Thanks Anne! Let me try to summarize which things use microtasks and which things don't in the current state of this PR:\r\n- Update selectedcontent synchronously:\r\n  - selected option element insertion via post-insertion steps\r\n  - selected option end tag parsed during HTML parsing\r\n  - selectedcontent element insertion via post-insertion steps\r\n  - option selectedness changes via script or user interaction\r\n- Update selectedcontent after a microtask:\r\n  - selected option removal\r\n  - selected option moving\r\n  - selectedcontent removal\r\n  - selectedcontent moving\r\n\r\nI think this sounds good to make and avoids inconsistencies/flakiness with parser yielding because moving/removal during parsing sounds unlikely to occur.\r\n\r\n> One thing we could consider doing still I think is doing the clone synchronously, but the insertion asynchronously, but that still leaves us with the problem of running the clone for the option element too early as we want to clone when we see the end tag, not when we've only seen the start tag.\r\n\r\nSo we would clone the option element immediately and store it in a document fragment variable that goes along with the microtask, then replace the contents of the selectedcontent element with the cloned stuff after the microtask? I don't really see the benefit of doing this since I'm worried that you might be able to find a way to run script within moving/removing steps as a result of cloning into the document fragment, and because it wouldn't solve the flakiness issues with parsing since replacement is still not always happening at the same time?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/4847695312/reactions",
